    "fg_answers": {
      "type": "fg-straight-answers",
      "blocks": {
        "sa_get": {
          "type": "row",
          "settings": {
            "q": "What you actually get",
            "a": "<p>Three workbooks — <strong>Harm OCD</strong>, <strong>Pure-O &amp; Rumination</strong>, and <strong>Scrupulosity</strong> — each supplied twice, as a print edition and a fillable edition.</p><p>Plus a cut-out sheet of <strong>8 pocket cards</strong>: one line each, no labels and no logo, so there is nothing to explain if someone sees one.</p><p><strong>268 pages</strong> in total — 137 print, 131 fillable — across 134 pages of unique material.</p>",
            "open": true,
            "flag": false
          }
        },
        "sa_rules": {
          "type": "row",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Using this without it turning into a compulsion",
            "a": "<p>A fair worry, and one most self-help material ignores completely. Three rules for how these are meant to be used:</p><ul><li><strong>Read each page once.</strong> Re-reading it for the version that finally feels right is the compulsion wearing a study hat.</li><li><strong>Set a timer.</strong> Twenty minutes, then stop, whether or not it feels finished. Stopping while it is still unresolved is the exercise, not a failure of it.</li><li><strong>Never re-do a worksheet to get it right.</strong> There is no right. A messy first answer has already done its job.</li></ul><p>And the thing these pages will never do: tell you whether your particular fear is true. Nothing can, and anything offering to is selling relief that expires within the hour.</p>",
            "open": false,
            "flag": true
          }
        },
        "sa_covers": {
          "type": "row",
          "settings": {
            "q": "What these three cover — and what they don't",
            "a": "<p><strong>They are for:</strong> frightening thoughts about harm you would never want; replaying and mental reviewing that happens entirely in your head, where there is no compulsion anyone can see; and guilt about faith, honesty, or whether you are a good person.</p><p><strong>They are not for:</strong> contamination, checking, symmetry and just-right, health anxiety, relationship doubt, or real-event and false-memory themes. Those are separate workbooks — <a href=\"/pages/learn\">find the one that matches</a> rather than making these fit.</p><p>Themes move around. The mechanics on these pages do not, which is why they still work when the subject changes.</p>",
            "open": false,
            "flag": false
          }
        },
        "sa_tried": {
          "type": "row",
          "settings": {
            "q": "I've tried worksheets before and nothing changed",
            "a": "<p>Most worksheets ask you to weigh the evidence and argue the thought down. With OCD that backfires — examining the content is itself a compulsion, so the relief expires like every other kind, and the thought comes back carrying more authority than before.</p><p>Nothing in these pages asks you to disprove a thought, rate how likely it is, or gather evidence for and against it. The work is practising moving before the doubt clears.</p>",
            "open": false,
            "flag": false
          }
        },
        "sa_after": {
          "type": "row",
          "settings": {
            "q": "What happens the second you pay",
            "a": "<p>Your files are on the order confirmation page immediately — nothing ships, nothing is posted, and there is no account to create. A backup link also goes to your email in case you close the tab.</p><p>On a phone they save to Files or Downloads like any other PDF. You can come back and download them again whenever you want.</p>",
            "open": false,
            "flag": false
          }
        },
        "sa_print": {
          "type": "row",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Can I print it, or fill it in on my phone?",
            "a": "<p>Both. Every workbook comes twice — a print edition and a fillable edition — so you can write by hand or type, and switch whenever you like.</p><p>When printing, choose <strong>100% or Actual Size</strong> rather than Fit to Page. Scaling is the usual reason margins come out clipped.</p>",
            "open": false,
            "flag": false
          }
        },
        "sa_therapy": {
          "type": "row",
          "settings": {
            "q": "Is this therapy?",
            "a": "<p>No. These are self-practice materials built from published ERP methods — the same approach a therapist would work through with you, on paper, at your own pace. They are meant to support working with a professional, not to replace it.</p><p>If you are in crisis right now, this is not the right tool for today. Please reach a person who can help.</p>",
            "open": false,
            "flag": false
          }
        },
        "sa_wrong": {
          "type": "row",
          "settings": {
            "q": "What if something's wrong with the file?",
            "a": "<p>Wrong file, missing pages, or it will not open on your device — email and you will get a working copy. Within 30 days, no argument. A real person reads every message.</p>",
            "open": false,
            "flag": false
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
